A||N||6||Chuck Essen||Radiator Replacement||||8||
Z||000000||Chuck Essen||08-28-2000||03:27 PM||||Radiator is leaking on 84 coupe. People are talking to me about a "Modine" replacement. Costs more than I can get elsewhere. Are they worth the extra cost? Can a shop that is not familiar with the fiero really screw up the repair? Get back to me asap. Thanks||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||08-28-2000||05:19 PM||||Personal (limited) experience:  I've replaced original-equipment  heater cores with Modines in various cars, including Fieros.  Seem to work just fine.<BR>No idea whether Modine is better or worse than any of the others brands - but it <B>is</B> a name I've heard of.  Opinion: I'd go for a "known" brand, and possibly a warranty on the replacement part.<BR>  <BR>Experience: I've replaced radiators in (a few)front wheel drive cars.  The job is so easy it hurts.  Opinion: The Fiero radiator <B>looks</B> even easier.  How could they (a shop)screw it up?  Never had to do one myself, so please chime in, members, if I am wrong  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !<P>Opinion: The easiest way for a mechanic unfamiliar with Fieros to mess up a radiator R & R job, would be failing to properly "burp" the cooling system.<P>Remember, too - radiators <B>can</B> be repaired.  Might want to price that option.<P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ! ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||08-28-2000||05:27 PM||||The Fiero radiator is a very simple job.  You'll need to remove the hood, but even that's not a hard thing to do.  <P>Have you looked into a good used radiator?  I don't know what that shop is charging you, but it may be a lot cheaper to get a used one.<P>I have several good used radiators if you need one (cheap!).  (A shameless plug, I know!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-28-2000||07:28 PM||||mrfiero--I need a new radiator.  Mine is developing a crack down one side (probably the other but I haven't looked).  '85 GT auto if it matters.  What's the price including shipping to 46992?  And did you ever find that drivers side seat cover you were looking for?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-28-2000||11:25 PM||||Shop Modine in the online parts stores as well as autozone pep boys or what ever.<P>Modine is a very well established company. They only sell new product. No referbs. Modine is about the only thing I use. (It's rare when they don't have what I want.) Everything I've seen from them has met and usually exceeded OE spec.<P>This is a job you can do yourself with very basic tools. The only area you need be a bit carfull with is connecting automatic transmission cooler lines. You need to use 2 wrenches to tighten the lines. (Ideally they should be "flair nut" wrenches. These get a better bite on the fittings than open end wrenches. You can get them at sears or many car parts stores. Makes jobs like fuel lines/filter easier too so you'll use them more than once.)<P>The rest is just nuts bolts and a screw driver or 2.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-28-2000||11:30 PM||||Is the tranny cooler a separate but integral part of the radiator?  Or does the antifreeze from the radiator used to cool the tranny?  I'm confused.  (I know.  What's new, right?)||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||08-29-2000||12:04 AM||||Monkeyman,<P>On auto. Fieros the trans cooler is integrated into the radiator tank.....the coolant cools the tranny fluid.  Therefore you need a radiator from an auto. (you can use an auto. radiator in a manual, but not vice versa).<P>I'm sending you an e-mail.  ||||reg||10
